ANALYSIS
================================================================================

🏆 Performance Impact (compared to correct implementation):

dynamic_system:
  • TTFT: -41.8% (faster)
  • Total Time: +52.3% (slower)
  • Lost Cached Tokens: 9,728

shuffled_tools:
  • TTFT: -37.1% (faster)
  • Total Time: +41.9% (slower)
  • Lost Cached Tokens: 12,288

dynamic_profile:
  • TTFT: -26.4% (faster)
  • Total Time: +44.1% (slower)
  • Lost Cached Tokens: 10,240

sliding_window:
  • TTFT: -33.7% (faster)
  • Total Time: +8.0% (slower)
  • Lost Cached Tokens: 6,656

text_format:
  • TTFT: -5.0% (faster)
  • Total Time: +504.6% (slower)
  • Lost Cached Tokens: -209,664

📈 TTFT Progression (first 5 iterations):
  correct             : 6.05s → 5.19s → 5.22s → 5.04s → 15.02s
  dynamic_system      : 3.52s → 3.90s → 17.71s → 6.64s → 10.25s
  shuffled_tools      : 3.80s → 4.90s → 3.93s → 7.57s → 31.62s
  dynamic_profile     : 4.46s → 3.69s → 9.09s → 9.19s → 26.22s
  sliding_window      : 4.01s → 5.25s → 5.07s → 11.05s → 14.06s
  text_format         : 5.75s → 5.12s → 8.20s → 14.08s → 8.55s

📝 Key Insights:
  1. The correct implementation maintains stable context for optimal KV cache usage
  2. TTFT improves dramatically after first iteration when cache is utilized
  3. Dynamic system prompts invalidate the entire cache on each request
  4. Shuffling tools breaks cache even though the functionality is identical
  5. Dynamic user profiles add unnecessary context changes
  6. Sliding windows may seem to reduce context but actually harm cache efficiency
  7. Text formatting breaks the structured message format that enables caching

